<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>7.8.3: Using Diagrams to Find Equivalent Quadratic Expressions</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01871</md:content-id>
    <md:title>7.8.3: Using Diagrams to Find Equivalent Quadratic Expressions</md:title>
    <md:uuid>a860ef95-560f-4bfa-a75a-245dabb2c12d</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Activity</title>

<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>Here is a diagram of a rectangle with side lengths \(x+1\) and \(x+3\). Use this diagram to show that \((x+1)(x+3)\) and \(x^2+4x+3\) are equivalent expressions.</para>
    <para><figure><media alt="Large rectangle, divided into 4 smaller rectangles. Left rectangle on top labeled x, Right rectangle on top labeled 3. Top rectangle on left side labeled x, bottom rectangle on left side labeled 1."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/d46e7dac037c0d1d77f1d1e4bb75d9727476ca83"/></media></figure></para>
  
  <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work. </para>
  
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  
  <para><figure><media alt="Area model multiplying x+3 and x+1"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/f7fc78e091f6ece205f3e5d15d21e2a30472cc68"/></media></figure></para>
  
  <para>\(x \cdot x =x^2\)
    \(1 \cdot x=x\)
    \(x \cdot 3=3x\)
    \(1 \cdot 3=3\)</para>
  
  <para>Adding these together gives \(x^2+4x+3\).</para>

</solution></exercise>
</example>
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>Draw diagrams to help you write an equivalent expression for each of the following:</para>
    <para>\((x+5)^2\) </para>
      <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work. </para>
  
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  
  <para>The diagram should show partial products that add up to \(x^2+10x+25\).</para>

</solution></exercise>
</example>
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para> \(2x(x+4)\) </para>
      <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work. </para>
  
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  
  <para>The diagram should show partial products that add up to \(2x^2+8x\).</para>

</solution></exercise>
</example>
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para> \((2x+1)(x+3)\) </para>
      <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work. </para>
  
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  
  <para>The diagram should show partial products that add up to \(2x^2+7x+3\).</para>

</solution></exercise>
</example>
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>\((x+m)(x+n)\) </para>
      <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work. </para>
  
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  
  <para>The diagram should show partial products that add up to \(x^2+xn+mx+xn\) or \(x^2+(m+n)x+mn\).</para>

</solution></exercise>
</example>
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>Write an equivalent expression for each expression without drawing a diagram. Use the distributive property to help. Remember FOIL for multiplying binomials: Multiply the First terms, the Outer terms, the Inner terms, and the Last terms.</para>
    <para> \((x+2)(x+6)\) </para>
      <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work. </para>
  
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  
  <para>\(x^2+8x+12\)</para>

</solution></exercise>
</example>
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>\((x+5)(2x+10)\)</para>
      <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work. </para>
  
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  
  <para>\(2x^2+20x+50\)</para>

</solution></exercise>
</example>

