<exercise data-wait-for-event="RFM1">
  <problem>
    <title>Extending Your Thinking</title>
    
    <para>Image 1</para>
    
    <para><figure><media alt="Square, side length = x. 5 rectangles, width = x, length = 1. 6 squares with side length = 1."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/c5b15617ab51a34141305b88d49a53bb6a7757a5"/></media></figure></para>
    
    <para>Image 2</para>
    
    <para><figure><media alt="Rectangle decomposed into smaller squares and rectangles"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/b7cb24d8f86dc740773265298778e6c195ac0045"/></media></figure></para>
    
    <para>Are Image 1 and Image 2 equivalent? Explain or show your reasoning.</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>Yes, because \(x \cdot x+5(1x)+6(1)=(x+2)(x+3)\).</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--End interaction-->

<exercise data-wait-for-event="RFM1">
  <problem>
    <para>What does this tell you about an equivalent expression for \(x^2+5x+6\)?</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>This shows that an equivalent expression is \((x+2)(x+3)\).</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--End interaction-->

<exercise data-wait-for-event="RFM1">
  <problem>
    <para>Is there a different non-zero number of 1-by-1 squares that we could use instead that would allow us to arrange the combined figures into a single large rectangle?</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>We could use four 1-by-1 squares. This would give the expression \(x^2+5x+4\). The rectangle would be \((x + 4)(x + 1)\).
      
      <figure><media alt=""><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/5d64e5540c5f24105b1a4349cd0d352d8cfdc110"/></media></figure></para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
